<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/euclidean-geometry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Euclid's <em>Elements</em> (c. 300 BCE) built an entire universe from five innocent axioms —
    "a straight line can be drawn between any two points", and four more. Everything else,
    including results you'll use in engineering and physics, is <strong>proven</strong>: each
    statement follows logically from earlier ones. Geometry is where humanity invented the idea
    that truth can be earned, step by visible step.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a classic proof, honestly constructed — the isosceles triangle theorem" widget="equationStep" config={proofCfg} />

  <h2>The five axioms, and the famous fifth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Axioms 1–4</strong> (lines, extension, circles, right angles) feel self-evident; the fifth — parallel lines never meet — feels like it should be provable.</li>
    <li><strong>It isn't.</strong> Twenty centuries of failed attempts ended in the 1800s with non-Euclidean geometry, where the fifth axiom is replaced and perfectly consistent universes result. Einstein's spacetime is one of them.</li>
    <li><strong>The method outlived the content:</strong> definitions, axioms, theorems, proofs — the structure every mathematical subject in this ladder still uses.</li>
    <li><strong>Construction tools are theorems in disguise:</strong> bisecting an angle is proving something exists, not just drawing it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Euclid, Elements (c. 300 BCE); Greenberg, Euclidean and Non-Euclidean Geometries.</p>
</TopicPage>
